  [202] Configuring Maximum Duration for Vercel Functions
  [204] The maximum duration configuration determines the longest time that a function can run. You can configure the maximum duration for Vercel Functions.
  [205] Consequences of changing the maximum duration
  [206] Changing the maximum duration gives an invocation more time before Vercel terminates it. With fluid compute , Vercel can reuse function instances and process multiple invocations in the same instance with optimized concurrency . Provisioned memory is billed for running instances, not as a separate memory allocation for every request. Active CPU billing applies while your code is executing, and pauses while your function is waiting on I/O. To learn more, see how pricing works .
  [207] For this reason, Vercel has set a default maximum duration for functions, which can be useful for preventing runaway functions from consuming resources indefinitely.
  [208] If a function runs for longer than its set maximum duration, Vercel will terminate it. Therefore, when setting this duration, it's crucial to strike a balance:
  [211] Maximum duration for different runtimes
  [212] The method of configuring the maximum duration depends on your framework and runtime:
  [255] Extended max duration Beta
  [258] Extended max duration is supported for the following runtimes during the beta:
  [266] Extended max duration is supported for projects using Secure Compute or Static IPs . BYOC does not support durations above 800 seconds during the beta.
  [289] Setting a default maximum duration
  [290] While Vercel specifies defaults for the maximum duration of a function, you can also override it in the following ways:
  [293] From the left side, open Functions in the sidebar. If your project uses fluid compute , select Advanced Settings . Then scroll to the Function Max Duration section.
  [294] Update the Default Max Duration field value and select Save .
  [295] The dashboard sets the project default. During the extended max duration beta, use per-function configuration for durations above 800 seconds.
  [334] Maximum duration of a deployment
  [335] To check the max duration that a specific deployment's functions run with, run vercel inspect with the --json flag against that deployment's URL:
  [338] The JSON output lists each function's max duration as timeout and its memory size as memorySize . The default vercel inspect output doesn't show max duration or memory, so include --json . To find out what is useful to check in this output, see Check function configuration in Debugging slow Vercel Functions. To view a deployment's function memory size in the dashboard instead, see Viewing your function memory size .
  [344] Hobby
  [356] The 800 second maximum is generally available for Pro and Enterprise teams. For beta requirements and examples, see extended max duration .
